.TH FLIGHTLAB 2
.SH NAME
flightlab: run, parse \- Danby three-dimensional flight models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "flightlab.m";
flightlab := load Flightlab Flightlab->PATH;

err := flightlab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, err) := flightlab->parse(spec);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Flightlab
is the Danby three-dimensional flight viewer as a library the model
calls:
.PP
.EX
flightlab->run(ctxt, "soccerkick", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.B eq
\(em plus
.B title
and
.BR manual .
.PP
The state is fixed at six values, in this order: downrange position,
lateral position, height, and then the three velocity components in the
same order.  The viewer plots height against downrange (the side view)
and lateral against downrange (the plan view), and ends the flight when
the height crosses zero from above, so the order is a requirement rather
than a convention.
.I Flightlab
says so, with an error from
.B run
or
.BR parse ,
rather than letting a mis-ordered spec look plausible on the screen.
.PP
Unlike the flat families a parameter here may have a negative minimum
\(em an aim or a crosswind is signed \(em so a slider runs from the
spec's own minimum rather than from zero.
.PP
The drag and Magnus terms that
.B danby/ballistics.b
and
.B danby/atmosphere.b
used to compute for these models are written out in the specs.  They are
three lines of arithmetic each, they are the physics the model is about,
and a reader should not have to open a second file to see them.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the buttons are
Tk buttons, the status line is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk
geometry manager's; the two views share one
.IR panel (9)
widget holding an image the library draws into.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/flightlab.b
